Docker provides multiple networking drivers, including bridge, host, none, and overlay.

The bridge network is the default networking mode for standalone containers. Containers attached to the same user-defined bridge network can communicate with each other using container names as DNS hostnames.

For example, an API container can connect to a PostgreSQL container using the hostname "postgres" if both containers belong to the same user-defined network.

Port publishing is different from container-to-container communication. The command "-p 8080:3000" maps port 8080 on the host to port 3000 inside the container. It does not change the port on which the application listens internally.

Host networking removes network isolation between the container and the host. In this mode, the container shares the host network namespace, and port publishing is unnecessary.

Overlay networks are primarily used to enable communication between containers running on different Docker hosts, such as in a Docker Swarm environment.

A common troubleshooting mistake is assuming that exposing a port automatically makes a container accessible to every other container. Containers on separate isolated bridge networks cannot communicate directly just because their ports are published.

